WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.200
<v Instructor>So now, let's talk about</v>

2
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.570
the different EC2 instance types.

3
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.600
So you have a lot of them and if you click on this link,

4
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.940
you will have great details about them.

5
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.790
But they can be combined into these categories,

6
00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.370
general purpose, compute optimized, memory optimized,

7
00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.150
storage optimized, accelerated computing, and HPC optimized.

8
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.180
And there is a naming convention in AWS.

9
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.360
So for example, m5.2xlarge.

10
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.870
M corresponds to the instance class.

11
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.444
Five is the generation because overtime,

12
00:00:30.444 --> 00:00:33.930
AWS is going to improve and release new instance types.

13
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.770
And 2xlarge is the size within the instance class.

14
00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:38.970
You don't need to know them,

15
00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.290
but if you're curious about them,

16
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.560
go to this link and you'll get a lot more information.

17
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.390
Well, we'll talk about a few different type

18
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.223
of instance types.

19
00:00:46.223 --> 00:00:48.600
So you have general purpose, and these are great

20
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.060
for diversity of workloads such as web servers

21
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.130
or code repositories.

22
00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.320
They will have a good balance between compute,

23
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.090
memory, networking.

24
00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:57.990
And so in this course,

25
00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.970
we'll be using general purpose instances.

26
00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:01.530
We'll be using the T2 Micro,

27
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.730
which is the three tier general purpose type of instance.

28
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.230
On the website that I just showed you,

29
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.930
you will see all the different types of instance

30
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.250
that are general purpose.

31
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.810
And this is gonna evolve over time,

32
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.100
so I won't update this slide,

33
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.380
but you can always refer back to the AWS website

34
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.480
to check what the instances are

35
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.330
in the general purpose type of family.

36
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.490
Then we have compute optimized.

37
00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.710
And these are instances that are great

38
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.620
and optimized for compute intensive tasks.

39
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.920
So what requires a high level of processor?

40
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:32.753
Well, for example,

41
00:01:32.753 --> 00:01:34.980
it could be if you're batch processing some data,

42
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.750
if you're doing media transcoding,

43
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.820
if you need high performance web servers,

44
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.610
if you're doing high performance computing called HPC,

45
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.020
if you're doing machine learning,

46
00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.510
or if you have a dedicated gaming server.

47
00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.710
So all these things are tasks that require a very good CPU,

48
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.000
a very good compute side.

49
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.730
And so EC2 instances

50
00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.670
do have this kind of particularity.

51
00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.140
And for now, all the compute optimized instances in EC2

52
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.840
are of the C name.

53
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.420
So C5, C6, and so on.

54
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.320
Next, we have some EC2 instances that are memory optimized,

55
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.530
and they are going to have a really fast performance

56
00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.700
for the type of workloads

57
00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.940
that will process large data sets in memory.

58
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.200
So memory means RAM.

59
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.400
And so the use cases

60
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.500
are this is going to be high performance

61
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.645
for relational or non-relational databases

62
00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:27.600
that are mostly going to be in memory databases,

63
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.580
distributed web scale cache stores.

64
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.680
So for ElastiCache, for example.

65
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.570
In memory databases that are optimized

66
00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.180
for business intelligence or BI,

67
00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.940
and applications performing real-time processing

68
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.640
of big unstructured data.

69
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.970
So in terms of the names for the memory optimized instances,

70
00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.480
there's going to be the R series because R stands for RAM,

71
00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.450
but there's also going to be X1, high memory, and Z1.

72
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.940
Again, you don't need to remember the name of the instances,

73
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.250
but good to know at a high level.

74
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.083
Okay.

75
00:02:57.083 --> 00:03:00.480
And finally, we'll have storage optimized instances.

76
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.640
They're great when you are accessing

77
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.210
a lot of data sets on the local storage.

78
00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.640
And so the use cases for storage optimized instances

79
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.380
are going to be high frequency

80
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.200
online transaction processing, so OLTP systems,

81
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.420
relational and NoSQL databases.

82
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.560
And we'll see those in details

83
00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.270
when we get to the database sections.

84
00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.270
Cache for in-memory databases, for example, Redis,

85
00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.720
data warehousing application, distributed file systems,

86
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.750
and the storage optimized instances in AWS

87
00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.497
will start with an I, a D, or H1.

88
00:03:30.497 --> 00:03:32.490
But again, you don't have to remember this.

89
00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.320
I'm just giving you examples.

90
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.850
So what does it mean?

91
00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.740
Let's compare a few instance types.

92
00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.500
So for example, for t2.micro, we have one vCPU

93
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.800
and one memory, one gigabytes of memory.

94
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.610
And if you look, for example, at r5.16xlarge,

95
00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:52.110
we have 16 vCPU and 512 gigabytes of memory.

96
00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.110
So we can see there's a lot of more emphasis on the memory.

97
00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:59.580
If we compare it, for example, to a c5d.4xlarge

98
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.480
we can see we have 16 vCPU and 32 gigabytes of memory.

99
00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.570
So less memory, more CPU, and so on,

100
00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:07.980
different network performance,

101
00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.660
different EBS bandwidth and so on.

102
00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.000
So just to give you a point of comparison,

103
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.230
and if you wanted a website

104
00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.200
to compare all the EC2 instances together,

105
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.160
there's one that I really like.

106
00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.380
It's called ec2instances.info,

107
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.450
and I'll show it to you right now.

108
00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.970
So I am on the ec2instances.info website.

109
00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:24.990
And as we can see,

110
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.980
we have a list of all the instances available in AWS.

111
00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:29.813
So really a lot.

112
00:04:29.813 --> 00:04:30.900
We also get some information

113
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.820
around the Linux on demand cost,

114
00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.110
the Linux reserves cost, and so on.

115
00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.640
So some cost information.

116
00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.940
We get information around the memory, the number of vCPU.

117
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.980
We can order by name, we can search it.

118
00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.170
So it's quite handy and I really like this website.

119
00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.420
And if you go and use AWS,

120
00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.790
you probably will use this website as well.

121
00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.200
So that's it for this lecture.

122
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.043
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

